{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3037","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3037","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3037\",\"parent_revision\":\"10eca737309b13fc319247d6f83288a7432430f602f5d7ba3a3b84c9f2e5ab04\",\"root_revision\":\"10eca737309b13fc319247d6f83288a7432430f602f5d7ba3a3b84c9f2e5ab04\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nA pointer sentence (\\\"see `X.md`\\\", \\\"load `Y.md` before...\\\") should state only two things: what the target is (identity) and when to reach it (trigger). When a pointer also restates facts, rules, or procedure steps that the target file itself explains, the duplication is worse than a gap \u2014 it costs tokens twice, drifts out of sync, and (per observation this session) an agent that reads a pointer summarizing the target often skips opening the target, believing it already knows what's there.\\n\\nThis was first caught in `AGENTS.md`'s pointer to `.claude/rules/skill-substitution.md`, which repeated the target's own opening sentence almost verbatim (fixed). This issue is the result of sweeping the rest of the repo for the same pattern.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Method\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nA 22-batch haiku-worker fan-out (Workflow tool) read all 107 source files with backtick-quoted `.md` file references (every `SKILL.md`, agent file, and command file with such a reference, plus `AGENTS.md`, `.claude/CLAUDE.md`, and all 34 `.claude/rules/*.md`), located every pointer sentence in each, read the full referenced target, and flagged any pointer that restated the target's content. That raw pass returned 110 candidates.\\n\\n**I then manually re-reviewed all 110 against a stricter bar before filing this issue.** Roughly 70 were false positives: a pointer naming the *topics* a reference file covers (e.g. \\\"see `X.md` for Console constructor parameters, styles, and color formats\\\") is a legitimate routing hook for multi-reference skills (`typer/SKILL.md`, `rich/SKILL.md`, `textual/SKILL.md`, `skill-creator/SKILL.md`, etc. all use this pattern deliberately so an agent picks the right file among several) \u2014 that is scope-naming, not content duplication, and should **not** be changed. Only pointers that restate an actual rule, fact, default value, procedure, or diagram \u2014 the kind of thing that lets a reader believe they know the mechanic without opening the target \u2014 are listed below.\\n\\n**Do not fix these inline from this issue without re-reading each target file first** \u2014 a pointer's wording can look redundant out of context but only the target's actual current content proves it, and file content may have moved since this sweep (2026-08-19).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Group A Pointer Restates The Target S Actual Content 30 Findings\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n| # | Source | Target | Current hook | Suggestion 1 | Suggestion 2 |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | `.claude/CLAUDE.md:84` | `.claude/rules/fact-verification-first.md` | \\\"Prompt names a specific product, version, or release event \u2014 run `WebSearch` FIRST before planning. See [Fact Verification First](./rules/fact-verification-first.md)\\\" | See [Fact Verification First](./rules/fact-verification-first.md) when prompt mentions specific products, versions, or release events. | Load [Fact Verification First](./rules/fact-verification-first.md) before planning if the prompt names a product or release. |\\n| 2 | `.claude/rules/review-and-correction-discipline.md:75` | `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/references/instruction-hygiene.md` | \\\"**Mechanism leaks** in task prompts, skills, and task files \u2014 the prompt is what a real user would type; schema, paths, model tier, and skill names live in the executor config. Provide CLEAR + CoVe instructions for the executing agent, not the system's implementation. See instruction-hygiene \u00a71\u20132.\\\" | **Mechanism leaks** \u2014 load instruction-hygiene \u00a71-2 before designing prompts and executors. | **Mechanism leaks** \u2014 see instruction-hygiene \u00a71-2 for system/task isolation patterns. |\\n| 3 | `AGENTS.md:333` | `writing-for-agents` skill | \\\"Before creating or updating Beads, skills, or persistent agent memories, load `writing-for-agents`; write ordered steps with checkable, exhaustive completion criteria and keep each shared reference in one source.\\\" | Before creating or updating Beads, skills, or persistent agent memories, load `writing-for-agents` for guidance on organizing work. | Load `writing-for-agents` before creating or updating Beads, skills, or persistent agent memories \u2014 it covers structuring work with clear completion criteria. |\\n| 4 | `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/SKILL.md:12` | `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/references/mcp-connection-check.md` | \\\"The `references/mcp-connection-check.md` file loaded by this skill is a plain file, not substituted \u2014 it shows bare SAM CLI subcommands and args only (e.g. `plan list`), never the invocation prefix. Prepend the command in `<sam_cli/>` above to every one of them.\\\" | See `references/mcp-connection-check.md` when MCP server tools fail to connect. | Load `references/mcp-connection-check.md` for MCP server troubleshooting and fallback procedures. |\\n| 5 | `plugins/development-harness/skills/codebase-auditor/SKILL.md:28` | `plugins/development-harness/docs/plugin-deployment-model.md` | \\\"Read `plugins/development-harness/docs/plugin-deployment-model.md` for the full gotchas and constraints. Key points: [4 bullets about sys.path constraints, bundle isolation, cross-boundary utilities]\\\" | Load `plugins/development-harness/docs/plugin-deployment-model.md` before modifying plugin scripts to understand deployment constraints. | See `plugins/development-harness/docs/plugin-deployment-model.md` for the zip-and-move test and what survives installation. |\\n| 6 | `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md:352` | `.../references/migration-fidelity-gate.md` | \\\"Execute the full gate procedure defined in [...]. **Summary of detection signals** (full evaluable criteria in the reference): - Issue title or body contains: 'migrat', 'convert format', ... - Any task `acceptance_criteria` field contains: 'delete', 'remove source', ...\\\" | Before proceeding to Artifact Discovery, execute the full migration fidelity sign-off gate defined in [./references/migration-fidelity-gate.md](./references/migration-fidelity-gate.md). | Detect migration signals and confirm fidelity items per [./references/migration-fidelity-gate.md](./references/migration-fidelity-gate.md) \u2014 blocks completion if any item unconfirmed. |\\n| 7 | `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md:382` | `.../references/concerns-processing.md` | \\\"Execute the full procedure defined in [...]. **Summary**: Read backlog item \u2192 if `## Concerns` has unchecked items, verify each (create backlog item if real; mark unconfirmed if not) \u2192 update section \u2192 proceed to Quality Gate Plan Creation. If no concerns section, proceed immediately.\\\" | Before Quality Gate Plan Creation, process accumulated concerns per [./references/concerns-processing.md](./references/concerns-processing.md) \u2014 verify unchecked items or skip if none present. | Verify and check off concerns from the backlog item following [./references/concerns-processing.md](./references/concerns-processing.md) \u2014 blocks proceed if verification needed. |\\n| 8 | `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md:665` | `.../references/recursive-follow-up-handling.md` | \\\"Execute the full follow-up routing procedure defined in [...]. **Summary**: - Step 2: Derive a search slug ... - Step 3: Classify scope ... - Step 4: Link follow-up plan ... - Step 5: Recursion Gate \u2014 Guards ... then Conditions ...\\\" | Route follow-up plans from the code review verdict to backlog per [./references/recursive-follow-up-handling.md](./references/recursive-follow-up-handling.md) \u2014 applies depth and RT-ICA guards before recursing. | Execute Steps 2\u20135 follow-up routing procedure (backlog search, scope classification, linking, recursion gates) from [./references/recursive-follow-up-handling.md](./references/recursive-follow-up-handling.md). |\\n| 9 | `plugins/development-harness/skills/file-classification/SKILL.md:13` | `plugins/development-harness/skills/multi-perspective-review/references/verdict-schema.md` \u00a72.5 | Full \\\"Tier Decision\\\" mermaid diagram + \\\"Tier Rules\\\" section reproduced inline instead of pointing at verdict-schema.md's own tier decision tree | For file classification rules and tier definitions, refer to [verdict-schema.md \u00a72.5](../multi-perspective-review/references/verdict-schema.md) \u2014 defines the authoritative tier decision tree. | Classify prose files by tier per [verdict-schema.md \u00a72.5](../multi-perspective-review/references/verdict-schema.md) before applying SKIP rules for any perspective. |\\n| 10 | `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md:18` | `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/references/mcp-connection-check.md` | \\\"**MCP server availability**: ... Both servers initialize in ~1\u20132 seconds after a session restart. Claude Code handles connection waiting automatically. If a tool is unavailable, see [mcp-connection-check.md](...) for troubleshooting.\\\" | If an MCP tool is unavailable, see [mcp-connection-check.md](../backlog/references/mcp-connection-check.md) for troubleshooting and fallback procedures. | Refer to [mcp-connection-check.md](../backlog/references/mcp-connection-check.md) when either SAM or backlog MCP servers fail to connect \u2014 covers initialization, diagnostics, and adapter selection. |\\n| 11 | `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/claude-plugins-reference-2026/SKILL.md:186` | `plugins/plugin-creator/docs/skill-subdirectory-warning.md` | \\\"[Skill subdirectory warning](...) \u2014 nested skill directories silently fail to register, with a wrong/right example; read before organizing related skills into grouping subdirectories.\\\" | See [Skill subdirectory warning](../../docs/skill-subdirectory-warning.md) for skill directory structure constraints before organizing your skills. | Read this [Skill subdirectory warning](../../docs/skill-subdirectory-warning.md) before structuring how you organize skill directories. |\\n| 12 | `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/claude-skills-overview-2026/SKILL.md:232` | `plugins/plugin-creator/docs/skill-subdirectory-warning.md` | Same duplication as #11, second instance, plus an added clause about \\\"Automatic Discovery from Nested Directories\\\" | See [Skill subdirectory warning](../../docs/skill-subdirectory-warning.md) to understand how skill directories must be organized. | Read [Skill subdirectory warning](../../docs/skill-subdirectory-warning.md) before planning your skill directory structure. |\\n| 13 | `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/claude-subagent-reference/SKILL.md:75` | `.../references/tool-and-permission-control.md` | \\\"Use `Agent(worker, researcher)` syntax to restrict which subagent types can be spawned. See [./references/tool-and-permission-control.md](...)\\\" | Use `Agent(worker, researcher)` syntax in `tools` to restrict which subagent types can be spawned. See [./references/tool-and-permission-control.md](./references/tool-and-permission-control.md) for context on `Agent()` restrictions. | To restrict spawnable subagent types, see [./references/tool-and-permission-control.md](./references/tool-and-permission-control.md) \u00a7 Restrict which subagent types can be spawned. |\\n| 14 | `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/optimize-claude-md/SKILL.md:108` | `.../references/index-discipline.md` | \\\"For CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md: read `.../index-discipline.md` before analyzing \u2014 this is the index routing reference; CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md are indexes, not encyclopedias\\\" | Load before optimizing CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md \u2014 establishes the index routing model and audit scoring. | Read first when refactoring index files to understand proper router entry format and content extraction. |\\n| 15 | `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/optimize-claude-md/SKILL.md:112` | `.../references/claude-rules-extraction.md` | \\\"For CLAUDE.md: read `.../claude-rules-extraction.md` before analyzing; perform path-scoped rules extraction phase after optimization analysis, before CoVe \u2014 path-scoped content (Python rules, CI yml, TypeScript) goes to `.claude/rules/`, not `docs/`\\\" | Load when identifying path-scoped rules for extraction \u2014 covers file-type detection and glob patterns. | Read to determine which CLAUDE.md sections should extract to `.claude/rules/` vs `docs/` destinations. |\\n| 16 | `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/prompt-optimization/SKILL.md:526` | `.../references/example-claude-md-boris-cherny.md` | \\\"> `references/example-claude-md-boris-cherny.md` \u2014 well-structured configuration demonstrating numbered workflow principles, actionable bullet points, balanced guidance with scope limiters, and self-referential learning loops.\\\" (near-verbatim copy of the target's own \\\"Analysis: What This Example Does Well\\\" section headings) | See `references/example-claude-md-boris-cherny.md` for a complete, real-world CLAUDE.md configuration with detailed analysis. | For inspiration on structuring CLAUDE.md files with practical patterns, consult `references/example-claude-md-boris-cherny.md`. |\\n| 17 | `plugins/python-engineering/skills/designing-ui-for-cli/SKILL.md:40` | `.../references/register-decision.md` | \\\"apply the four-step first-match-wins decision rule in `./references/register-decision.md`. Default `product-cli` when nothing matches.\\\" (the default value is step 4 of the target's own rule) | Apply the first-match-wins decision rule in `./references/register-decision.md` to determine register. | Use the decision rule from `./references/register-decision.md` \u2014 first match wins, product-cli default. |\\n| 18 | `plugins/python3-development/skills/analyze-test-failures/SKILL.md:14` | `.../references/python3-standards.md` | \\\"Consult `.../python3-standards.md` when shared testing or quality rules from this plugin apply; full standards, graphs, and amendment process are documented there.\\\" | Load `../python3-development/references/python3-standards.md` when shared testing rules from this plugin are referenced. | Check `../python3-development/references/python3-standards.md` to understand plugin-wide testing and quality standards. |\\n| 19 | `plugins/python3-development/skills/comprehensive-test-review/SKILL.md:21` | `.../references/python3-standards.md` | \\\"Consult `.../python3-standards.md` when auditing against shared testing and quality rules. The checklist below summarizes this skill's focus; it does not replace the full standards document.\\\" | Load `../python3-development/references/python3-standards.md` before defining custom coverage targets or mocking patterns beyond this checklist. | Refer to section 1.8 of `../python3-development/references/python3-standards.md` for complete testing standards and error handling patterns. |\\n| 20 | `plugins/python3-development/skills/create-feature-task/SKILL.md:14` | `.../references/python3-standards.md` | Same \\\"full standards, graphs, and amendment process are documented there\\\" boilerplate | Load `../python3-development/references/python3-standards.md` before finalizing architecture decisions or CLI interface design. | Reference `../python3-development/references/python3-standards.md` section 1 for layered architecture, module hygiene, and exception handling patterns. |\\n| 21 | `plugins/python3-development/skills/hatchling/SKILL.md:14` | `.../refer